# CrewOS — every credential and knob the project can use.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      then fill in only what the rung you are running needs
#
# NOTHING IN THIS REPO LOADS .env AUTOMATICALLY. It is Node stdlib only, no dotenv,
# on purpose — one less thing to install at 5:55pm. To actually apply it:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a      # bash/zsh — then run ./run-demo.sh in that shell
#   node --env-file=.env orchestrator/server.js     # Node 20+, single process
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit a filled-in copy, and never paste one into chat,
# a screenshot, or a demo terminal that is being projected.
#
# =============================================================================
# WHAT EACH RUNG ACTUALLY NEEDS  (docs/demo-script.md has the full table)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   rung 1  ./run-demo.sh fake   nothing at all. No values below are required.
#   rung 2  ./run-demo.sh        a logged-in `claude` CLI. No values below.
#   rung 3  ./run-demo.sh live   CREW_BACKEND=google + the two Google files.
#   rung 4  ./run-demo.sh voice  rung 3, plus VoiceOS Pro on the demo Mac.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. REAL CREDENTIALS — the only genuinely secret things here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Google (Gmail + Calendar) ------------------------------------------------
# These are FILES, not env vars, because that is what the Google client libraries
# want. Both are gitignored. Neither exists yet — this is the project's one
# outstanding credential blocker.
#
#   voiceos-bridge/demo-seed/credentials.json   OAuth client secret, downloaded
#                                               from console.cloud.google.com
#   voiceos-bridge/demo-seed/token.json         written on first authorise;
#                                               seed.py and the google backend
#                                               share it, so you authorise once
#
# How to make credentials.json: voiceos-bridge/demo-seed/README.md, step 5.
# Use a THROWAWAY Google account. seed.py's --wipe calls batchDelete, which is
# permanent, and it acts on whatever account token.json happens to hold.

# Override where the google backend looks for the cached token. Leave unset to
# use demo-seed/token.json, which is what authorising once gives you.
#CREW_TOKEN=

# --- VoiceOS ------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is NO env var and NO API key. VoiceOS is a desktop app; you sign in inside
# it, and every participant of this hackathon gets a free month — redeem that
# rather than starting a paid trial. Its live auth tokens sit in:
#
#   macOS    ~/Library/Application Support/VoiceOS/config.json
#   Windows  %APPDATA%\VoiceOS\config.json
#
# NEVER commit, paste, or screenshot that file. `config.json` is gitignored
# repo-wide for exactly this reason. Read it with register.ps1, which prints only
# an allowlist of keys and never dumps the file.

# --- Claude -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The orchestrator spawns the `claude` CLI, which uses your subscription login,
# not a key. Run `claude` once interactively and sign in. Rung 1 needs none of it.
# Only set this if you are deliberately driving the CLI off an API key instead.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. WHICH MAILBOX THE TOOLS ACT ON
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# fake   in-memory, seeded from demo-seed/fixtures.json. No account, no network.
#        Real archiving, real booking, real numbers — this is a panic button, not
#        a stub, and it is the default so an unconfigured machine still demos.
# google the real demo account, via token.json above.
CREW_BACKEND=fake

# The phone does NOT follow CREW_BACKEND, and that surprised me, so it is worth
# stating here next to the switch people expect to govern it.
#
#   mailbox : fake unless you ask for google  -> safe by default
#   phone   : REAL unless you set DRY         -> live by default
#
# So `./demo real` on a machine with CREW_PHONE set rehearses against a fake
# mailbox and a real handset. `./demo` now sources this file, so CREW_PHONE is
# populated on every run, and execution-direct.md actively tells agents to ring
# rather than guess a missing detail — which is right, and means rehearsals will
# call more often, not less. B wrote the reason for the kill switch already: a
# loop that really texts a phone thirty times gets the number rate-limited or
# the human numb to it.
#
# Uncommented here on purpose, so the TEMPLATE is safe the way CREW_BACKEND is.
# **Delete this line for the real run, or the phone will not ring on stage.**
A1MOBILE_DRY=1

# Where the fake backend persists its mailbox between calls (VoiceOS respawns the
# server, and in memory alone an archive would undo itself between agent steps).
#CREW_STATE=voiceos-bridge/mcp-server/.crew-mailbox.json


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. HOW THE AGENTS ACT  (set by run-demo.sh — override only to debug)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# narrate  agents talk, touch nothing            <- safe, the plan of record
# direct   agents call the crew_* tools          <- the mailbox really changes
# voice    agents speak commands to VoiceOS      <- the whole point of the event
#CREW_MODE=narrate

# Skip Claude entirely and use canned narration. The whole show, zero spend.
#FAKE=1

# Tool allowlist passed to `claude -p`. mcp__crew__<tool> — Claude Code's naming,
# not the bare tool name and not VoiceOS's custom_mcp_ prefix.
#   node voiceos-bridge/mcp-server/mcp-config.js --tools
#ALLOWED_TOOLS=

# Hard kill for a hung agent. The character says "ran out of time" rather than
# freezing.
#AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS=180000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. PACING AND VOICES  (measured — see coordination.md before changing)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Milliseconds between narration lines. 4000 is a MEASUREMENT, not a preference:
# a line takes 2.8-4.3s to actually speak, and at 1400 and again at 2200 lines
# were silently dropped. If you change CREW_RATE, re-measure this.
#LINE_MS=4000

# Content lines per agent before the "Done:" sign-off. The show's length is
# (total lines x LINE_MS) and nothing else.
#MAX_LINES=3

# Words per minute for the dock's narration.
#CREW_RATE=200

# Silence the dock's narration entirely. Bubbles still update.
#CREW_MUTE=

# Which output device the dock narrates to. This is the audience channel and it
# MUST NOT be the loopback device, or VoiceOS hears the narration and the voice
# loop breaks. run-demo.sh sets it; an unknown name warns and falls back to the
# built-in speakers rather than going silent.
#CREW_AUDIO_DEVICE=MacBook Pro Speakers

# One accent each, so the crew reads as three entities rather than one robot.
# Download the Enhanced builds in System Settings — same names, no code change.
#CREW_VOICE_TRIAGE=Moira
#CREW_VOICE_SCHEDULER=Daniel
#CREW_VOICE_RECAP=Karen


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. WIRING  (defaults are correct; change only if a port is taken)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#PORT=4001                                        # orchestrator listens here
#DOCK_PORT=4002                                   # dock listens here
#DOCK_URL=http://localhost:4002/agent-status      # orchestrator -> dock
#ORCH_URL=http://localhost:4001                   # MCP bridge -> orchestrator
#TIMEOUT_MS=8000                                  # bridge's fetch cap
#CREW_LOG=voiceos-bridge/mcp-server/crew-bridge.log   # JSONL audit of every call


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. TESTS ONLY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#PHRASE=clean up my inbox and schedule everything  # test-stdio.js trigger phrase
#REQUIRE_DONE=1                                    # demand the status loop reach
                                                   # "the crew is finished"
                                                   # (stub orchestrator only)
